*Thinking...*

> I'm thinking through what an ideal single diagnostic turn should look like for this "Perfection" system—a compiler-like artifact generator. The core question is whether the existing apparatus of pillar ledgers, banners, footers, and manifests is actually necessary or just accumulated cruft that should be stripped away for a cleaner design.
> 
> The key realization is that each turn should be almost entirely question-driven, with minimal status overhead. A user needs to know what they're being asked, why their answer matters, what their options are, what happens if they skip it, and roughly how much remains—but structural questions are inherently closed-set by design, so the ideal presentation is a short enumerated choice rather than an open prompt. That's a massive UX improvement because answering becomes recognition instead of composition.
> 
> The second insight is batching: if the question set is mostly derivable upfront from analyzing the artifact, why ask turn-by-turn at all? Root questions are largely independent and can be asked together, with dependent questions following in a second turn—collapsing the typical flow to 1–2 turns instead of 3–5. The real win is the "proposed reading" pattern: rather than asking cold questions, I present what I've read and ask for confirmation or correction, converting N questions into one confirmation with N inline escape points. That's dramatically lighter than an interrogation.
> 
> But there's a risk: confirm-or-correct can invite lazy assent where the user says yes without reading, which violates the no-invention principle in spirit. So I should only pre-answer where the material actually determines the answer—binding, not inventing. Where genuinely undetermined, I ask openly. The turn splits into two zones: what I read from their material (bound, correctable, not phrased as a question) and what I genuinely cannot tell (actual questions, closed-set, with consequence and default). The ideal structure is one line of orientation, Zone A with what I've read, Zone B with real questions, then one line on what happens next—no banners, ledgers, protocol restatement, or footers cluttering the flow.
> 
> The deleted elements—pillar ledger, status banner, manifest, protocol framing, notices—were solving problems that don't exist in a bounded process. The ledger was a progress bar for an unbounded loop; a bounded process needs at most "2 of 2" or stays internal. The banner gets replaced by a single terminal sufficiency statement. The manifest belongs at delivery with the artifact, not mid-diagnostics. Protocol framing moves to the first turn only, one sentence, or disappears entirely. Notices fold into the lines they qualify.
> 
> The three problems they solved—unbounded loops needing reassurance, unclear stopping points needing status, invisible reasoning needing audit trails—are all handled structurally now: bounded set, terminal sufficiency, and disclosed reading. Keeping the apparatus would be paying interest on a retired debt. But disclosure survives as a genuine requirement: defaulted immaterial values must be visible, just batched at delivery rather than per-turn.
> 
> For phrasing root questions, the core rule is to ask about the world, not the model—never "is this adjudicative or generative?" but instead "does this decide something about work someone submitted, or produce something new?" Frame it in the user's domain vocabulary, and each question should carry its consequence, because consequence makes an unfamiliar question answerable. I need concrete phrasings for the four root questions.
> 
> A concrete example: "when this runs, will a person be there to answer questions, or does it run unattended?" That's the right level of specificity. For sufficiency communication, one terminal statement saying what was resolved and what remains open by design—something like "Structurally complete. N decisions bound, M values left to supply at run time"—that signals *done* unambiguously, because the failure mode is the user not knowing if they can stop. Gates should be communicated at delivery as a short to-do list for the user, framed as cost—A3's twelve gates should look expensive. Every line of information needs to either enable an answer or change a decision.
> 
> I should test this with a concrete mock turn using a realistic artifact—say, a grant application reviewer prompt—and show what turn 1 looks like. I need to avoid jargon for someone who's never read the spec: no "tier," "gate," "hole," "materiality," or "binding." Translate gate to "you'll supply at run time," drop structural language entirely, and use "ready" instead of sufficiency. The format should be substantive but tight—headers, a mock turn, maybe a table for what to delete. I'm laying out two clarifying questions with radio-button options to pin down the scope: whether out-of-scope submissions follow the same review path or get a shorter rejection, and whether the reviewer needs to be available during execution or if everything must be decided upfront. I'm using the "your call" framing to let them pick the simpler path if they're unsure, and I'll note my assumptions in the artifact.
> 
> But I'm realizing there's a UX principle here: "your call" only makes sense for nested questions where a default exists—disjoint questions that block compilation need a real answer. So question 2 shouldn't offer that escape hatch. I should mark only question 1 with the default indicator and adjust the closing note to clarify that "your call" applies only where a default is shown.
> 
> I'm also thinking about turn expectations—most runs should be one round of questions plus delivery, occasionally two. And for internal state like ledgers and verification details, I should keep those hidden by default but make them accessible on demand through an audit view. The key insight is to demote the apparatus rather than delete it. I'm aiming for around 900 to 1100 words—their messages tend to be substantive, so a thorough answer fits, but I need to stay focused and not ramble.

## The consequence that reshapes everything else

Your structural questions are **closed-set by construction.** Nested candidates are a binary (branch exists / doesn't). Disjoint candidates are a small enumeration. Nothing in the diagnostic set is open-ended.

That changes the interaction from *composition* to *recognition*. The user is not being asked to describe their intent; they're being asked to point at one of two or three named shapes. Recognition is roughly an order of magnitude cheaper than recall, and it's the difference between "this feels like a form" and "this feels like a conversation with someone who read my draft."

Almost every UX gain in this phase comes from committing to that. Open prompts are now an *error*, not a style choice.

## The second consequence: this probably isn't turn-by-turn

The root questions are mutually independent. If they're independent and bounded, serialising them is pure latency. And most of them are *bindable from the supplied material* — which means the honest move isn't to ask, it's to **state the reading and invite correction**, reserving questions for what's genuinely undetermined.

That collapses the typical run to **one question turn plus delivery.** Two if a conditional question opens up.

A ledger that tracks progress across five turns is meaningless when there are two.

## The ideal turn

> I read your draft as a reviewer that scores conference submissions and returns a recommendation. Three things I could pin down from what you gave me; two I can't.
>
> **From your draft:**
> - Output is a written review with a numeric score *(from "return score and comments")*
> - It judges work someone submitted, rather than producing new work
> - Nothing requires outside facts — everything comes from the submission
>
> Correct any of those and I'll rebuild around it.
>
> **What I can't tell:**
>
> **1. Do out-of-scope submissions take a different path, or does everything get the same review?**
> - everything gets the same review ← *I'll build this if you skip*
> - out-of-scope gets a separate short path
>
> **2. When this runs, will someone be available to answer questions, or does it run unattended?**
> - someone's there
> - unattended — anything undecided has to be settled now, or supplied as input before it starts
>
> Say *"your call"* on 1 and I'll take the marked option and tell you I did. 2 I can't guess — it changes what's possible to build at all.

Nine lines of substance. No banner, no ledger, no protocol restatement, no footer, no jargon.

## Anatomy

**One orientation line.** What I understood, in the user's vocabulary. This is doing the work your framing paragraph used to do, at a twentieth the cost, and it doubles as an error-detector: if the reading is wrong, the user interrupts before answering anything.

**A bound-reading block.** This is the disclosure requirement discharged *at the moment it's cheapest* — while the user is already reading. Source hints matter: "*(from "return score and comments")*" is what distinguishes binding from inventing, visibly, without ever using either word.

**The questions, with consequence attached.** A user who has never read the spec cannot answer "is this adjudicative?" but can always answer "does it judge submitted work, or make something new?" The rule is: **ask about the world, never about the model.**

**Defaults marked only where the architecture permits them.** This is the detail I'd most insist on. Nested questions carry a marked default; disjoint questions carry none. The user learns the nested/disjoint distinction *tacitly*, from the shape of the choice, and never hears the words. UX derived from the architecture instead of describing it.

## Your explicit test: how much is remnant?

Most of it. And it's remnant for a traceable reason — the apparatus solves three problems the architecture just retired.

| Apparatus | Problem it solved | Why it's now dead | Disposition |
|---|---|---|---|
| Pillar ledger | unbounded loop → user needs reassurance progress exists | bounded set; ≤2 turns | **demote** to on-request |
| Status banner | no stopping condition → user needs to infer state | sufficiency is terminal and stated once | **delete** |
| Per-turn notices | scattered defaults, no single disclosure point | bound-reading block + delivery disclosure | **fold in** |
| Protocol framing | user didn't know what kind of process this was | one orientation line, first turn only | **delete after turn 1** |
| Gate manifest | cost invisible | real requirement — wrong location | **move to delivery** |
| Tier/materiality/routing state | auditability | never changed a user's next action | **internal, on request** |

**Demote, don't delete** is the right verb for most of it. `why did you ask that?` and `show your reading` should surface the full internal state on demand. Auditability survives; the per-turn tax doesn't.

The one thing that must not be lost is **disclosure of defaulted immaterial values** — that's load-bearing for no-invention. But it belongs batched at delivery, as a short "I chose these; change any," not sprayed across turns.

## Sufficiency and gates

**Sufficiency needs to be unmistakably terminal**, because the failure mode is a user who doesn't know they're allowed to stop:

> Done — nothing structural left to decide. Two things you'll supply when you run it: the submission text, and the score threshold.

**Gates are the user's to-do list, not system state.** Frame them as *what you will need to have on hand*, in the user's words, counted. A twelve-gate artifact should read as expensive:

> This needs twelve things supplied every time it runs. That's a lot — if some of those are always the same, tell me and I'll fix them in place.

That last clause is worth more than the disclosure itself. It turns cost visibility into a repair opportunity, which is the thing that actually prevents A3-style artifacts from shipping.

## The density rule

One test, applied per line: **delete it — does anyone answer differently or do anything differently?** If no, it's boilerplate. Status about the system's own process almost always fails this test; consequence attached to a choice almost always passes.

## Open UX questions I'd want to settle next

1. **Does confirm-or-correct invite lazy assent?** A user who skims the bound-reading block and answers only the questions has effectively let inference bind structure. My instinct is that source hints plus restricting the block to genuinely determined items is enough — but this is the highest-risk thing in the design and it should be tested, not reasoned about.
2. **Do disjoint questions with no default feel like being blocked?** The honest framing ("this changes what's possible to build") may read as helpful or as stonewalling depending on wording.
3. **Turn 1 for a user who has never seen the system** — how much orientation is needed before the reading block lands, and does it survive being cut to one line?

I'd start with (1). It's the only one where a UX choice can breach an architectural guarantee.
